# ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
# TOON relay box nginx config.
# Serves: proxy.relay.DOMAIN (connector ILP edge)  relay-ws.DOMAIN (relay app)
# NOT the faucet: it gets its own box, with no connector on it (toon-meta
# `docs/two-node-architecture.md` §4 — that doc lives in toon-meta, not this
# repo; connector#898 tracks the faucet box here).
#
# Written to point directly at connector-rust:4000 — NOT at a TypeScript
# connector. infra/linode-store/nginx/node.conf.template used to name
# "http://connector:3000" (the retired TypeScript node), predating that
# box's own cutover; fixed in issue #819 to match its rendered
# nginx/conf.d/node.conf. This box has no TypeScript connector at all, so
# there was never a stale value to carry over here.
# ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=relay:10m rate=30r/s;

map $http_upgrade $connection_upgrade {
    default upgrade;
    ''      close;
}

# Port 3100 (the relay app's payment-oblivious write backend) is deliberately
# absent from this map and from every location below. It must never be
# publicly reachable — infra/linode-store/nginx/conf.d/node.conf:198-228
# records what happened the one time a payment-oblivious app's path was left
# open on this fleet: it was a free door (#778).
map $host $backend {
    default                  "";
    proxy.relay.${DOMAIN}    "http://connector-rust:4000";
    relay-ws.${DOMAIN}       "http://relay:7100";
}

server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;
    server_name proxy.relay.${DOMAIN} relay-ws.${DOMAIN};

    location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ {
        root /var/www/certbot;
    }
    location / {
        return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
    }
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    listen [::]:443 ssl;
    http2 on;
    server_name proxy.relay.${DOMAIN};

    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/proxy.relay.${DOMAIN}/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/proxy.relay.${DOMAIN}/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

    resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=10s ipv6=off;

    # ── Every upstream below names its container through a VARIABLE ─────────
    # (issue #993, and the correct form for issue #987)
    #
    # nginx resolves a LITERAL upstream hostname exactly once, at
    # config-PARSE time, and caches that address for the life of the worker.
    # Docker hands a recreated container a NEW address on the compose
    # network, so the moment anything recreates an upstream -- the
    # label-scoped Watchtower of toon-meta#403 recreating it on a new
    # `:release` digest, or a plain `docker compose up -d` -- this edge
    # answers `502 connect() failed (111: Connection refused)` until someone
    # runs `nginx -s reload`. That is an unsupervised outage on a box whose
    # whole point is that a green merge reaches it with no operator in the
    # loop, and it is what took the store edge down on 2026-08-16.
    #
    # Naming the upstream through a variable moves resolution to REQUEST
    # time, where the `resolver` directive above (Docker's embedded DNS, 10s
    # TTL) applies -- so a recreate self-heals within the TTL, no reload.
    # Proven live on both boxes by forcing an upstream onto a new address:
    # relay `swap-node` .7 -> .8, `/swap/ilp/btp` back to `101` in 3s; store
    # `connector-rust` .4 -> .8, `/ilp/identity` back to `200` in 3s. Neither
    # needed a reload.
    #
    # THE URI RULE (issue #987 as filed; CORRECTED by issue #1023). A variable
    # upstream does not do the literal form's prefix substitution -- but nginx
    # does NOT ignore a URI part on one either. It honours it: nginx's own
    # proxy_pass docs say "if URI is specified in the directive, it is passed
    # to the server as is, replacing the original request URI", and #1023
    # measured exactly that on nginx 1.18 through 1.31. So
    # `proxy_pass http://$u:3400/ilp/btp;` does send `/ilp/btp`. #987 read the
    # maker's 404 as "the appended URI is ignored and the original
    # `$request_uri` is forwarded"; that is not what nginx does, and the 404
    # had another cause -- a rendered `conf.d` that had drifted from its
    # template would produce it, and #999 records these files drifting apart.
    #
    # What IS true, and why the form below is still the right default: a URI
    # part on a variable upstream is STATIC. It replaces the WHOLE request
    # URI, so neither the path past the location nor the query string survives
    # it -- silently, and reading the same in the file either way. The form
    # both boxes run keeps both: `rewrite ^.*$ /<target> break;` to set `$uri`,
    # then a proxy_pass with NO URI part at all -- a variable proxy_pass
    # without a URI forwards the (possibly rewritten) `$uri` AND the args.
    # Never append `$uri`/`$request_uri` to the proxy_pass itself; that
    # double-applies against the rewrite, and `$uri` drops the args on its own.
    # Where the upstream path already equals the request path
    # (`/ilp/identity`) no rewrite is needed.
    #
    # Test any change here over HTTP/1.1 (`curl --http1.1`): HTTP/2 strips
    # the `Upgrade` header and masks a broken WebSocket upstream.
    #
    # A second, older reason for the same form (issue #985 finding 2): an
    # unresolvable literal upstream is a hard `[emerg] host not found in
    # upstream` that exits the whole nginx master process, taking every
    # server block in this file down -- not just the location that names a
    # service nobody has brought up yet. A variable defers that to a 502 on
    # the one path.

    # The whole /admin prefix is refused here rather than proxied — mirrors
    # both other boxes (infra/linode-store/nginx/conf.d/node.conf,
    # infra/linode-node/nginx/conf.d/node.conf). 404 rather than 401/403 so
    # the refusal does not advertise that an admin surface exists at all.
    # `^~` outranks any regex location.
    location ^~ /admin { return 404; }

    # The retired /rust/ prefix (ADR 0013) — this box never ran a parallel
    # fleet of its own, but the guard costs nothing and keeps this file's
    # shape identical to the other two boxes' if that prefix is ever probed
    # here by habit.
    location ^~ /rust/ { return 410; }

    # ── CORS for the liveness read, and ONLY the liveness read ─────────────
    # Mirrors infra/linode-store/nginx/conf.d/node.conf's identical block:
    # the public dashboard is served from the apex origin and derives each
    # node's up/down dot from `GET /ilp/identity`, a cross-origin fetch for
    # this box without this header (toon-meta#267). Scoped to an
    # EXACT-match location and to ONE origin, deliberately — `/ilp/identity`
    # is already public and unauthenticated and returns only this
    # connector's public signing key, but every other path here stays
    # same-origin-only.
    location = /ilp/identity {
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin "https://proxy.${DOMAIN}" always;
        add_header Vary "Origin" always;
        limit_req zone=relay burst=400 nodelay;
        set $upstream connector-rust;
        proxy_pass http://$upstream:4000;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
    }

    # ── rolling-swap MAKER sidecar (issue #983, connector-infra half of
    #    toon-meta#402) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # A path under THIS box's own domain/certificate, not a dedicated
    # subdomain — the maker needs no DNS record or TLS cert of its own,
    # unlike toon-meta#402's original enumeration of human steps (see
    # docs/operators/swap-node-bringup.md). `swap-node` resolves by
    # container-name DNS once docker-compose.relay.swap.yml's service joins
    # this compose project's default network, same as `connector-rust`
    # above. Exact-match `location =`, ahead of the generic `location /`
    # below (nginx checks exact matches first regardless of file order, but
    # grouping them together here mirrors `/ilp/identity`'s own placement).
    #
    # Direct-dial model (swap#105): a client reaches the maker by dialing
    # `/swap/ilp/btp` from its OWN kind:10032 announce
    # (docker-compose.relay.swap-announce.yml), never through this box's
    # `connector-rust` routing table — there is no `[[routes]]` entry
    # forwarding to it and there should not be.
    #
    # These two locations use the variable-upstream form the block by
    # `resolver` above explains, and they were the first here to need it for
    # its OWN older reason: this compose service does not run until a human
    # brings it up (docker-compose.relay.swap.yml's header), and a literal
    # upstream nobody has started yet is a hard `[emerg] host not found in
    # upstream` that exits the nginx master and takes the connector edge and
    # relay-ws down with it. As committed the form was still wrong -- the
    # appended `/ilp/btp` was silently ignored and the maker 404'd on the
    # unstripped `/swap/ilp/btp` (issue #987); the `rewrite ... break` below
    # is what actually strips the prefix. Verified live at `101 Switching
    # Protocols` over `--http1.1`.
    location = /swap/ilp/btp {
        set $upstream swap-node;
        limit_req zone=relay burst=60 nodelay;
        rewrite ^.*$ /ilp/btp break;
        proxy_pass http://$upstream:3400;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;
        proxy_read_timeout 1h;

        client_max_body_size 4m;
    }

    # The maker's HTTP-over-ILP surface. The proven direct-dial swap flow
    # only ever dials BTP above; this exists because `[announce]
    # http_endpoint` is a REQUIRED, validated field (crates/connector-
    # config/src/announce.rs) that
    # `infra/linode-relay/connector-rust.swap-announce.toml` must name
    # something reachable for — an embedded `ConnectorNode`
    # serves both surfaces off one port, the same single-port shape this
    # box's own `connector-rust:4000` already uses (`/ilp` and `/ilp/btp`
    # side by side on the SAME upstream).
    location = /swap/ilp {
        set $upstream swap-node;
        limit_req zone=relay burst=60 nodelay;
        rewrite ^.*$ /ilp break;
        proxy_pass http://$upstream:3400;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;

        client_max_body_size 4m;
    }

    location / {
        if ($backend = "") { return 404; }
        limit_req zone=relay burst=60 nodelay;

        proxy_pass $backend;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
        # What TLS-terminates `wss://…/ilp/btp` for the future peering, and
        # what relay-ws.DOMAIN needs for the relay app's own websocket.
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;
        proxy_read_timeout 1h;

        client_max_body_size 4m;
    }
}

# ── relay-ws.DOMAIN — the relay app's own public websocket edge ──────────────
# A SEPARATE `server` block from proxy.relay above, for one reason: it presents
# a SEPARATE certificate lineage. Per #830 the two names are issued as two
# independent certbot requests (see init-letsencrypt.sh's CERT_NAMES), never as
# one multi-SAN request -- a SAN request is all-or-nothing, so bundling them
# means either name failing validation takes the other's certificate down with
# it. That is exactly how the relay box ended up unable to obtain any
# certificate at all before #830. Two lineages, two blind spots that cannot
# reach each other.
#
# This is also the far end of the DNS move in infra/devnet-manage.sh: post-#820
# `relay-ws.devnet` resolves HERE, and the apex no longer names it in any
# server_name or cert lineage (infra/linode-node/nginx/conf.d/node.conf).
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    listen [::]:443 ssl;
    http2 on;
    server_name relay-ws.${DOMAIN};

    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/relay-ws.${DOMAIN}/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/relay-ws.${DOMAIN}/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

    resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=10s ipv6=off;

    # Same two guards as the proxy.relay block above, for the same reasons and
    # to keep the two blocks' shape identical.
    location ^~ /admin { return 404; }
    location ^~ /rust/ { return 410; }

    location / {
        # `$backend` is the same `map $host` table at the top of this file, so
        # this name's upstream (relay:7100 -- the relay app's PUBLIC websocket
        # port, never the payment-oblivious :3100) stays declared in exactly
        # one place. The guard keeps an unmapped Host a 404 rather than a
        # proxy_pass to the empty string.
        if ($backend = "") { return 404; }
        limit_req zone=relay burst=60 nodelay;

        proxy_pass $backend;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
        # The relay speaks NIP-01 over a websocket: without these two the
        # upgrade never completes and every client sees a plain HTTP 400.
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;
        proxy_read_timeout 1h;

        client_max_body_size 4m;
    }
}
